openssl: seal/open without EVP_SealInit()/EVP_OpenInit()

EVP_SealInit() dumps core on this OpenSSL (3.6.5) inside its own
RAND_priv_bytes_ex() call, before any error can be reported: the crash
reproduces with a plain C program too, so it is not a PHP-side problem.

Do the seal by hand, with the same semantics the deprecated helpers had:
a random session key (RAND_bytes), the key PKCS#1-encrypted once per
recipient (EVP_PKEY_encrypt), then CBC-encrypt the payload with that key.
The IV is all-zero, matching what EVP_SealInit() did for php here (php
passes iv = NULL and never returns the IV).

openssl_open() mirrors it: EVP_PKEY_decrypt() the session key, then
EVP_Decrypt* the payload. Data sealed by older builds stays readable and
vice versa, because both sides use PKCS#1 v1.5 padding.

